3. KEYWORD CLUSTERING BY SEARCH INTENT
Analyse every supplied keyword and group keywords by meaning and intent, not just shared words.
Decide whether each keyword belongs on:
The homepage.
A category or hub page.
A supporting article or guide.
A separate calculator, converter, or tool page.
A comparison page.
An FAQ or subsection.
An existing page that should be improved.
A future page requiring further research.
No page, with a clear reason.
Create this table:
| Cluster | Primary keyword | Supporting keywords | Search intent | Recommended page type | Proposed URL |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
Rules:
Assign every supplied keyword a destination or explain why it is excluded.
Identify duplicates, close variants, and semantically related terms.
Combine keywords on one page when they share intent and can be served well together.
Recommend separate pages when intent, user task, required functionality, or expected content differs meaningfully.
Do not create a URL for every keyword variation.
Do not overload the homepage with every supporting keyword.
Flag irrelevant, overlapping, unsupported, or uncertain keywords.
Avoid assigning the same primary keyword to multiple pages without a clear reason.
Identify likely keyword cannibalisation risks.
10. Record each keyword's destination so coverage can be audited.
4. HOMEPAGE KEYWORD STRATEGY
Decide the homepage's primary purpose first. Normally, the homepage targets the main keyword and its closest intent-matching variations.
Provide:
One primary keyword.
Close variants.
Secondary semantic terms and important entities.
Main search intent and user task.
Unique value proposition.
Recommended title tag and meta description.
H1 and proposed H2/H3 outline.
Main tool or feature placement, if relevant.
Topics that belong on the homepage.
Topics that deserve separate supporting pages.
Contextual links from the homepage to those pages.
Rules:
Give the main keyword a clear, natural focus.
Do not target every supporting keyword equally.
Answer the primary user need early.
Add examples, FAQs, and related links only when useful.
Do not make the homepage unnecessarily long just to include every keyword.
Use the homepage as the central hub when that suits the site and user journey.
5. SITE ARCHITECTURE: HUB AND SPOKE
Build a clear hierarchy connecting the homepage, optional category hubs, supporting pages, tools, and trust pages.
Conceptual model:
Homepage / Main Tool
Core topic sections
Supporting cluster A
  - Supporting page A1
  - Supporting page A2
Supporting cluster B
  - Supporting page B1
  - Supporting page B2
Supporting cluster C
Trust and policy pages
Deliver:
A readable tree diagram of the proposed site.
A URL and page map.
A reason for every proposed page.
Each page's target keywords, intent, purpose, parent, and links.
Use this table:
| Page | Parent page | Proposed URL | Primary keyword | Page purpose | Priority |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
Rules:
Build the smallest useful structure that covers the topic well.
Avoid thin pages, doorway pages, and URLs created solely for search traffic.
Do not create empty category pages that add no navigation or content value.
Use descriptive, consistent, human-readable slugs.
Keep related pages together logically.
Preserve useful existing URLs where possible; recommend redirects only when justified.
Mark speculative page ideas as optional until their usefulness is validated.
Do not link to planned pages until they are live and useful.
6. TOPICAL AUTHORITY AND SEMANTIC COVERAGE
Map the core topic, subtopics, related entities, user questions, and content gaps.
Include:
Core topic and main user task.
Essential and supporting subtopics.
Related entities and terminology.
Beginner and advanced questions.
Comparisons and alternatives.
Practical examples and use cases.
Common errors and misconceptions.
Relevant official sources, standards, or regulations where applicable.
Topic gaps found in competitor research.
For each topic, state whether it belongs on the homepage, a dedicated page, an FAQ, an interactive feature, or should be excluded.
Rules:
Build meaningful coverage rather than publishing many low-value pages.
Use relevant entities naturally and accurately.
Explain relationships between concepts.
Use examples, demonstrations, and evidence when useful.
Address meaningful user needs, not just competitor word counts.
Never claim that a certain number of pages or keywords guarantees rankings or topical authority.
7. INTERNAL LINKING PLAN
Plan internal links before content production.
Create this matrix:
| Source page | Destination page | Suggested anchor text | Placement | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|---|
Rules:
The homepage links to important supporting pages where contextually relevant.
Every supporting page links back to its parent or main hub.
Related sibling pages link to one another when useful to readers.
Category hubs link to their relevant child pages.
Methodology and trust pages are linked from relevant tools, claims, and explanations.
Use descriptive, natural anchors that accurately describe the destination.
Vary anchors naturally; do not force exact-match anchors into every link.
Avoid excessive repeated links, irrelevant cross-links, and links added only to manipulate rankings.
Use crawlable HTML links.
10. Prevent orphan pages and unnecessary click depth.
11. Never link to pages that do not exist yet.
12. Keep breadcrumbs, navigation, related-page blocks, and contextual links consistent.
For each page, specify incoming links, outgoing links, parent page, sibling pages, breadcrumb path, and whether it belongs in the main navigation, footer, or contextual links. Do not automatically put every page in the main navigation.
