15. GOOGLE SEARCH CONSOLE CSV ANALYSIS
I may attach multiple CSV exports from Google Search Console (GSC). Analyse **all attached CSV files** before deciding keyword strategy, site architecture, country priorities, or language rollout.
For each file:
Identify the report type, date range, dimensions, metrics, and visible filters.
Inspect all rows; process large files programmatically rather than relying on a preview.
Handle different column names and export formats carefully, and state any uncertainty.
Combine files only when dates, dimensions, filters, and metrics are comparable. Avoid double-counting overlapping exports.
Preserve the source data and report missing columns, assumptions, and data-quality limits.
Analyse queries/keywords, pages, countries, clicks, impressions, CTR, average position, and trends where available. Identify query-to-page relationships, possible cannibalisation, high-impression/low-CTR queries, meaningful visibility opportunities, queries without a suitable page, and countries whose impressions suggest a language opportunity.
Provide a table where data supports it:
| Query or keyword | Page | Country | Clicks | Impressions | CTR | Average position | Recommended action |
|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
Also provide a country performance summary, query/page opportunities, mapping of existing queries to proposed clusters and URLs, pages to improve before creating new ones, and a prioritised action plan. Do not assume the highest-impression country is automatically the best market. Consider clicks, impressions, position, CTR, search intent, language, business relevance, localisation effort, and data quality. Never invent search volume, KD, revenue, or metrics absent from the files or reliable connected tools. Clearly distinguish GSC observations from recommendations.
